  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Urban Programme WASH - Gender and Basic Sanitation in Urban Favelas</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aims to address the lack of basic sanitation in favelas in S&#xe3;o Paulo, Brazil.

The Urban Livelihoods programme aims to improve access to housing and basic services, including sanitation, for poor and disadvantaged families living in the shanty towns, illegal settlements and derelict tenement blocks and on the streets of S&#xe3;o Paulo, Brazil&#x2019;s largest city, whilst also tackling the impacts of climate change on the city&#x2019;s poorest. 

This new project will specifically address the lack of access to basic sanitation in city peripheries and favelas (shantytowns) where the poorest men and women live. Vast inequality and the gap between rich and poor in Brazil is reflected in huge variations in access to water and sanitation, which disproportionately affects low-income women and female-headed households. 

This project will benefit low-income families, who will increase their participation in decision-making spaces and their capacity for community-based advocacy to access improvements in basic sanitation and better socio-environmental and living conditions. Women, often heads of poorer households, will be particularly targeted and supported to ensure their views, leadership and contributions in basic sanitation policies via participation in community organisations and public decision-making councils.</narrative>
  </description>
